course: ukrainian-street
lesson: 29
title: Vyshyvanka Day, midsummer, St Nicholas — and the polite guest
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: On a Thursday in May Sashko comes to school in a white shirt with red and black stitching: a vyshyvAnka, an embroidered shirt, treasured in Ukrainian families — "harna vyshyvAnka!", lovely shirt, is a compliment he'll love.
explain: "SlAva UkrayIni!" — Glory to Ukraine! — has a fixed answer, "HeroYam slAva!", Glory to the heroes!, and it is said with respect, never as a joke.
explain: On the night of St Nicholas, Den Svyatoho Mykolaya, kids find presents under the pillow; at midsummer, Ivana Kupala, girls float flower wreaths on the water.
explain: And the polite guest at Sashko's house: "DObryi den, pani ..." to his mum, "mozhna vzyaty?" before you touch anything, "vybachte, de tualEt?" when you need it, "DYAkuyu, bulo duzhe smachno" after the meal — she answers "na zdorOv'ya!" — and "DYAkuyu za hostynu" as you leave, so she says "pryKHOdy shche!".
fact: "vyshyvAnka" means an embroidered shirt, worn on special days and on Vyshyvanka Day, a Thursday in May; compliments on it are welcome.
fact: "SlAva UkrayIni!" means "Glory to Ukraine!", a patriotic greeting, and the fixed answer is "HeroYam slAva!", "Glory to the heroes!".
fact: "Ivana Kupala" is an old midsummer night festival with flower wreaths and bonfires, and young people jump over the fires; some families still mark it.
fact: "Den Svyatoho Mykolaya" means St Nicholas Day, now kept on 6 December in Ukraine; children find presents under the pillow, and in jest naughty ones get a twig.
fact: "DObryi den, pani ..." means "good day, Mrs ..."; "pani" is used for a woman and "pane" when calling a man.
fact: "DYAkuyu, bulo duzhe smachno" means "thank you, it was delicious", the best thing a guest can say after a meal.
fact: "na zdorOv'ya" means "you're welcome" when you thank someone for food, and also "bless you" after a sneeze; adults say it as a toast too.
fact: "mozhna vzyaty?" means "may I take it?", asked before touching a toy, a book or a sweet from the bowl; the answer is "bery!", take it!
fact: Taking things without asking looks rude to Ukrainian grannies.
fact: "de tualEt?" means "where's the toilet?", and it is politer with "vybachte" in front.
fact: "DYAkuyu za hostynu" means "thanks for having me", said when leaving the friend's house.
fact: "pryKHOdy shche!" means "come again!", said by the friend's mum as you leave, and you answer "DYAkuyu!".
exercise: Sashko wears a white shirt with stitched patterns on a Thursday in May. What is it? | A vyshyvAnka | A pYsanka | A shapka | A yalYnka
exercise: What is a kind thing to say about Sashko's vyshyvAnka? | harna vyshyvAnka! | smIshno! yak u babusi! | zhadIba! | ne lai!
exercise: Someone says "SlAva UkrayIni!". What is the fixed answer? | HeroYam slAva! | SlAvimo Yoho! | VoIstynu voskrEs! | na zdorOv'ya!
exercise: How should "SlAva UkrayIni!" be said? | With respect, never as a joke | Only as a joke, to make people laugh | Only in a whisper | Only to small children
exercise: What happens at Ivana Kupala? | A midsummer festival with flower wreaths and bonfires | A winter festival with presents | Easter with coloured eggs | New Year with a tree
exercise: On St Nicholas Day, where do children find presents? | Under the pillow | In a stocking by the fire | In their school bag | Under the yalYnka on 31 December
exercise: On which date is St Nicholas Day now kept in Ukraine? | 6 December | 25 December | 24 August | 1 January
exercise: You meet Sashko's mum in the morning. What do you say? | DObryi den, pani ... | pryVIT, pane, yak spravy? | zdorOv! | BUvai!
exercise: After dinner at Sashko's, what is the best thing to say? | DYAkuyu, bulo duzhe smachno | ya nayIvsya, BUvai | SMAchnoho! | ne mozhna!
exercise: You thank Sashko's mum for dinner. What does she answer? | na zdorOv'ya! | SlAvimo Yoho! | sam takYi! | ne lai!
exercise: You sneeze at Sashko's house and his granny says "na zdorOv'ya!". What does it mean here? | Bless you | You're welcome | Good night | Go home now
exercise: You want a sweet from the bowl on the table. What do you ask first? | mozhna vzyaty? | de tualEt? | kotra hodyna? | u tebe ye kit?
exercise: You ask "mozhna vzyaty?" and Sashko's mum says "bery!". What does she mean? | Take it! | Put it back! | Wash it first! | Not now, later!
exercise: You need the toilet at Sashko's house. What is the politest way to ask? | vybachte, de tualEt? | de tualEt?! | daI tualEt! | tualEt, zaraz, shvYdshe!
exercise: You're leaving Sashko's house after a party. What do you say to his mum? | DYAkuyu za hostynu | DObroho RANku | z NOvym rOkom | shche p'yat khvylYn
exercise: Sashko's mum says "pryKHOdy shche!" as you leave. What does she mean? | Come again! | Go home now! | Hurry up, it's late! | Don't forget your hat!
test: my mate says "SlAva UkrayIni" is a funny thing to shout as a prank. right? | No, it's said with respect, not as a joke | Yes, everyone laughs at it, it's an old meme online | Yes, it means "hello" | No, it's a swear phrase
test: Sashko's granny sees you walk in and grab a toy without asking. What does she think? | That it's rude: you should ask "mozhna vzyaty?" | That you're polite | That you're hungry | Nothing at all
test: Sashko gets a twig on St Nicholas Day and laughs. What's going on? | A jokey "you were naughty" present | A real punishment for a bad mark at school | A broken present | A gift for the yalYnka
test: You say "na zdorOv'ya!" when Sashko's mum serves the soup. What went wrong? | That's the host's answer to thanks, not what the guest says | Nothing, it's right | It means "I'm full" | It's a swear phrase
test: Sashko's mum says "na zdorOv'ya" when you sneeze, and your mate says she wished you a nice meal. What did she mean? | Bless you | Enjoy your meal | Go to bed now | Wash your hands
test: What would happen if you said "DYAkuyu za hostynu" as you left? | Sashko's mum would be delighted | She'd think you were rude | She'd ask you to stay the night | Nothing, nobody says it
test: You're asked "SlAva UkrayIni!" at a school assembly about Ukraine. What do you reply? | HeroYam slAva! | DYAkuyu! | SlAvimo Yoho! | VoIstynu voskrEs!
test: oi, where's the loo, says your mate at Sashko's. How should he say it there? | vybachte, de tualEt? | de tualEt?! shouted across the room | daI tualEt! | kotra hodyna?
test: You call Sashko's grandad "pani". What slip did you make? | pani is for a woman; to him you say "pane" | pani is for children only | pani means "granny" | No slip, pani is for men
test: At Ivana Kupala young people jump over a bonfire. Should a kid try it at home? | No, fire is dangerous: just watch | Yes, small fires cannot burn you at all | Yes, if a friend is watching | Yes, it brings good luck
test: The family wears vyshyvanky on a Thursday in May. Which day is it most likely? | Vyshyvanka Day | Independence Day | St Nicholas Day | Easter Sunday morning
exam: It is given that the salutation "SlAva UkrayIni!" has been addressed to a person. Identify the customary response. | HeroYam slAva! | SlAvimo Yoho! | VoIstynu voskrEs! | na zdorOv'ya!
exam: Select the expression which a guest should employ, upon the conclusion of a meal, to convey gratitude and appreciation of its quality. | DYAkuyu, bulo duzhe smachno | na zdorOv'ya | SMAchnoho | pryKHOdy shche
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the expression "na zdorOv'ya"? | It is the host's reply to thanks for food and also a response to a sneeze | It is a guest's request for further food | It is a valediction upon departure | It is a greeting reserved for Easter
exam: Ascertain the conduct prescribed for a guest who wishes to take an object within the household of another. | The guest should first enquire "mozhna vzyaty?" | The guest should take the object without enquiry | The guest should enquire "de tualEt?" | The guest should declare "DYAkuyu za hostynu"
